---
title: "The deck engine"
description: "How slides are cut from a flat document, what the runtime owns at view time, and what an embedded deck costs."
---

`format: deck` renders to a slide deck driven by Taliesin's **own** engine, not
reveal.js. The engine is two bundled assets, `assets/css/deck.css` and
`assets/js/deck.js`, emitted inline by `render/deck.rs`. Three decisions shape it:

- **A native DOM contract.** The markup is `.tali-deck > .tali-slides > section`
  (each slide a `.tali-slide`, with nested `<section>` stacks). Per-slide visibility
  is the camera transform itself, not state classes. The public JS surface is
  `window.TaliesinDeck` (`initialize` / `sync` / `slide` / `on` / `getSlides` /
  `getCurrentSlide` / `registerPlugin`, …), which the preview client and theme
  extensions bind to. It is the only name: the reveal-era alias was deleted.
- **Bundle everything offline.** `deck.css` / `deck.js` (and KaTeX, the `{js}`
  enhancer, highlight CSS) are `include_str!`'d into the page. No CDN, no network.
- **Reuse the block model.** Every slide's blocks still carry `data-block-id` +
  `data-sourcepos`, so the block diff, click-to-source, and live execution work in a
  deck exactly as on a page, *within the current slide*.

## From document to slides

Give `post.tmd` a `format: deck` line and the very same three blocks become a
one-slide deck: the `# Cooling coffee` heading opens the slide, with the math and
the live plot riding along unchanged. The deck is built from the same block list as
every other output, and `render/deck.rs` groups those blocks into slides:

```{mermaid}
flowchart TB
  blocks["block list<br/>(data-block-id + sourcepos)"] --> split["split_slides<br/>## → slide, # → section stack, --- → break"]
  split --> dedup["dedup_slug<br/>distinct section ids"]
  dedup --> thread["heading-attribute threading<br/>{background-* / auto-animate} hoisted onto &lt;section&gt;"]
  thread --> emit["deck_page_from_doc<br/>inline deck.css/js (+ KaTeX / {js} enhancer when present)"]
```

- **`split_slides`** walks the blocks: a level-2 heading opens a slide, a level-1
  heading opens a *section* whose following slides become a vertical stack, and a
  `---` rule is an explicit break. Each slide gets an id from its heading text,
  deduped (`step`, `step-1`, …) so repeated titles, common with auto-animate, don't
  collide in the DOM or the URL hash.
- **Heading-attribute threading.** Authors write per-slide options on the heading
  (`## Title {background-color="#0f172a" auto-animate=true}`). The renderer lifts
  those onto the `<section>` as `data-background-*` / `data-auto-animate`, where the
  runtime reads them.

## The runtime (`deck.js`)

A single `deck` state object holds the position (`h`, `v`, `frag`: horizontal,
vertical, fragment step), the mode, and the plugin/listener registries. The core
loop is small:

- **`layout`** scales the fixed 960×540 (16:9) stage to the viewport with a single
  CSS transform (`--tali-deck-scale`), so slides never reflow; `fitSlide` shrinks a
  slide's own font if its content overflows (PowerPoint-style autofit).
- **`apply`** updates the per-slide classes (fragment visibility, chrome),
  records the previous slide for auto-animate, and calls `setCamera`
  to pan and zoom the stage to the current slide. Visibility *is* that camera
  transform, not a per-slide `display` toggle (the contract from the top of this
  chapter).
- **Navigation** (`right`/`left`/`down`/`up`, plus keyboard and touch) moves through
  the step list, then across slides.

### One step list per slide

Fragments, code line-stepping, and magic-move all advance with the same key, so they
share one ordered step list. `fragsOf(slide)` returns step descriptors in document
order:

- `{frag: el}`: an incremental list item or `.fragment`.
- `{code: pre, seg}`: the next segment of a `code-line-numbers` block.
- `{mm: div}`: the next snapshot of a `.magic-move` block.

`deck.frag` is how many steps have been taken; advancing dispatches to the matching
handler. Magic-move and auto-animate animate with **FLIP** (measure First and Last
rects, Invert with a transform, Play), scale-corrected by `--tali-deck-scale` so the
motion is right inside the scaled stage.

The ordering is **document order, with the right granularity per device.** `fragsOf`
walks one combined selector (`.fragment, .incremental > ul > li, .incremental > ol > li,
pre[data-code-lines], .magic-move`) so every step descriptor lands in the order its
element appears in the slide. A code block contributes one step per `|`-segment *after* the first (segment 0
is the slide's base highlight, applied on entry before any step), and a magic-move
block contributes one step per block-to-block morph (`n` code blocks → `n-1` steps).
That is the whole sequencing rule: a fragment, a code-step, and a magic-move step all
weigh the same, and they interleave exactly as written. So a `## ` slide that opens a
`code-line-numbers` walk, drops a `. . .` pause, then a `.fragment` bullet will step
through the code first, then reveal the bullet, in source order, on the one key.

### Three step kinds, three render-time contracts

Each animated step kind is set up at render time so the runtime can drive it from
static markup, never re-rendering:

- **`code-line-numbers` stepping.** A `#| code-line-numbers: "1|2-3|all"` cell option
  (or a `code-line-numbers="…"` fenced attribute) makes the emitter wrap every code
  line in a `.tali-hl-ln` span and bake the spec onto the `<pre>` as
  `data-code-lines="1|2-3|all"` (`render/emit.rs`). At runtime each step calls
  `highlightLines(pre, seg)`: `parseLineSpec` reads a segment (`"2-3"`, `"1,4"`,
  `"all"`, or empty), adds `tali-hl-lines-active` to dim the block, and toggles
  `tali-hl-ln-hl` on the named lines. `all` or empty clears the dim. Stepping is pure
  class-toggling over the already-highlighted DOM, so it costs nothing and survives
  the block diff.

- **`magic-move` (FLIP between consecutive code blocks).** A `::: {.magic-move}` div
  holds two or more code blocks; `render/divs.rs` line-wraps each `<pre>` with the
  same `.tali-hl-ln` helper. On the step from block *k* to *k+1*, `morphMM` keys both
  blocks' lines by their trimmed **text**: a line present in both glides from its old
  rect to its new one (FLIP, scale-corrected by `--tali-deck-scale`), a line only in
  the new block fades in, and the old block fades out under it. So unchanged lines
  *move* rather than redraw, which is the whole point: the eye tracks the lines that
  stayed and reads only what changed.

- **`auto-animate` (FLIP between consecutive slides).** Writing
  `## Title {auto-animate=true}` on two adjacent slides bakes `data-auto-animate` onto
  each `<section>` (hoisted off the heading by `take_bg_attrs`, the same lift that
  moves `background-*` onto the section). When navigation crosses from one
  auto-animate slide to the next, `autoAnimateTo` matches elements by tag **plus**
  text (the headings `h1` to `h4`, plus `p`, `li`, `pre`, `blockquote`, `img`,
  `figure`), measures each
  pair's before/after rect, and FLIP-tweens the survivors (position and font-size)
  while unmatched elements just appear. The camera trick that makes it look in-place:
  the engine overlays the incoming slide on the *outgoing* slide's grid cell, morphs
  the matched elements there, then snaps both the slide and the camera to the real
  cell in one move, so the reposition is a net-zero screen change.

### Modes

One engine, several presentations, selected by a `?tali=` query param, a class, or the
viewport shape:

| Mode | Trigger | What it does |
|------|---------|--------------|
| normal | **default** — a deck opens as a deck, standalone or embedded | the interactive (stepped) deck — the presenting surface |
| speaker | `S` → popup at `?tali=speaker` | current + next slide previews, notes, timer |
| feed | `?tali=feed`, or **auto on a portrait / phone screen** | a vertical scroll-feed of full-screen slides (native scroll-snap), for reading a deck on a phone; each slide's `::: {.notes}` surface as reader narration under it (feed-only, CSS-gated on `html.tali-feed`) |
| overview | `Esc` / `O` | zoomed grid of all slides |

There is no `?tali=embed` mode: an embedded deck runs the *normal* interactive engine
inside its iframe (the iframe is the isolation boundary — see below), it is just
detected as framed (`window.self !== window.top`) so it follows the host's `data-theme`
and never auto-routes to the feed. A deck opens **as a deck** — there is no reader/scroll
mode and no PDF-export mode.
The shareable artifact is the live HTML deck; a stray `Ctrl/Cmd-P` falls back to a
minimal `@media print` that shows the current slide legibly (colours kept as-is, so a
dark deck prints dark rather than inverting to invisible ink). The **Share** menu tool
surfaces that artifact: it encodes `location.href` (which deep-links slide + step + live
`{{< input >}}` state) into an offline QR + copy-link. The QR encoder is a self-contained
~180 lines in `deck.js` (byte mode, ECC level L, versions 1–10; no CDN, works on
`file://`) — not a vendored library, so nothing to attribute. **Present-mode fullscreen**
also takes a `navigator.wakeLock` so the display can't dim mid-talk. Speaker view and the
audience window stay in sync over `postMessage` (it works on `file://`, unlike
`BroadcastChannel`). Overview keeps each slide's background, shown per tile.

## Embedding a deck

`{{< embed deck.tmd >}}` (see [Slides, books & sites](../guide/using/formats.html)) is a
built-in shortcode in `render/extension`. It emits an iframe to the referenced
deck's built `.html` plus a fullscreen button; the iframe is the isolation boundary
that keeps the deck's full-viewport CSS, global keyboard handler, and URL hash from
fighting the host page. The build and preview discover embed targets
(`embed_targets`) and render each referenced deck to its own self-contained page, so
the iframe resolves without the deck also becoming a navigable site page.

The iframe is not a convenience, it is the only honest way to host a deck inside a
page. A deck is built to *own the viewport*: it scales a fixed 960×540 stage to fill
the window, binds a document-level `keydown` handler for arrow-key navigation, reads
and writes `location.hash` for deep links, and listens for `postMessage` from a
speaker window. Drop that markup straight into a blog post and every one of those
assumptions becomes a bug: the full-viewport stage would blow out the article's
layout, the global key handler would swallow the reader's arrow keys mid-scroll, and
the deck's hash writes would clobber the page's own fragment. The iframe gives the
deck its own document, its own viewport, its own key handler, and its own hash, so it
behaves exactly as it does standalone while the host page stays untouched. The
embedded copy runs the *normal* interactive engine inside that iframe; it is just
detected as framed (`window.self !== window.top`), so it follows the host page's
`data-theme` rather than its own `localStorage` (a dark article frames a dark deck)
and never auto-routes to the phone feed.

The speaker view takes a different tack for its previews. Rather than a second live
deck, its current- and next-slide panes are static **DOM-clone snapshots** of the
slide `<section>`s (`snapshotInto`): the clone is scaled into the pane, its fragments
and code-steps are replayed to the right step, and any `<canvas>` bitmap is copied
across — so no second copy of the deck re-runs its `{js}`.
